First review: reconstruct and challenge an argument (@greek-first-review: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-socratic-inquiry, #philosophy @greek-nature-being, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-nature-being) { r_3879MMB9TCFTCMJ2SPYG32ZDS0
Choose an argument from a completed reading. Produce a short explanation, written or spoken, that another reader can check against the retained book.

- Identify the author, translation, passage, speaker, and question.
- State the conclusion and its supporting reasons. Name an assumption that matters.
- Explain one serious objection and how the speaker or a defender could respond.
- Compare a second passage that changes, supports, or challenges the account.
- State your own considered answer and one difficulty it still faces.

Use the [reading-note method](../foundations/Read-an-argument.lingua#r_4GMQCKPBF9BCSKDK0KPN013EBN) if reconstruction is difficult. Revisit [a disputed term](../foundations/Terms-and-translation.lingua#r_1VGPFMXZ4HHAREA3BAHTSSQQ8D) if the disagreement depends on its meaning. Use [the source distinction](../foundations/Socrates-and-his-sources.lingua#r_1WAA32AYYPKYVETNZ5JGZ2SHFT) if the account moves too quickly from character to historical author.

**Review criteria:** the reader can locate the evidence; the reconstruction is recognisable and charitable; reasons are distinguished from conclusions; the objection reaches a real premise or inference; the response addresses that objection; the stated limitation matches what has been shown. Agreement with Socrates is not a criterion.

**Transfer:** take a claim about expertise, law, learning, or persuasive communication from daily life. Reconstruct it using the same method and identify where the ancient comparison helps or breaks down.

Keep this work in your own notes or an ordinary Record linked to the guide. Reading completion, a mastery claim, and another person's assessment are different statements; explain the basis of any claim you choose to record.

**Choose next:** [Nature and being](../presocratics/Nature-and-being.lingua#r_61M87EN28QQ9AHXT80SPCB0CVH), [Plato and Aristotle](../Plato-and-Aristotle.lingua#r_6Z4HA576BYZD1JVJ66TJE0QQJE), [Schools of life](../schools/Schools-of-life.lingua#r_58ENCWSNQARKCDXEP8FVWY9507), or [a question across readings](../questions/Questions-and-crossings.lingua#r_1KWY32YG72JSYZZEYW3GH6W0XF). **Home:** [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).

**Continue the survey:** the [broad introductory route](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3) carries this method into the earlier thinkers, Plato, Aristotle, the schools, and later Platonism. Use [a Portuguese edition](../sources/Portuguese-editions.lingua#r_3DABDPGKRDSM682Q2NYDEVX4Z8) for the same argument where available.

**Next depth:** preserve this work as foundation evidence and continue to [readiness review](../research/Foundation-readiness.lingua#r_2P555101Z0VGGZ14M12HRWKSYY), [a researched debate](../research/Scholarly-inquiry.lingua#r_2FEBDJ8R5JDHXX54TSATY2CRVZ), and [the seminar families](../research/seminars/Seminars.lingua#r_5FTSQDJTR924S9Z0G9EDBQFR1Y).
